        "narration": "Khasi sounds nothing like Hindi, and that's because it comes from a totally different family of languages - one shared with places like Vietnam and Cambodia, far across Southeast Asia! <\n\nIt's written using the same alphabet as English, which is unusual for an Indian language - missionaries wrote it down for the first time less than 200 years ago.\n\nIn Khasi and Garo communities, family names pass down through mothers, not fathers - so when you meet someone in Meghalaya, their surname tells you about their mother's family line! =i=g"
