          "minAge": 3,
          "maxAge": 99
        },
        "narration": "In the rainy hills of Meghalaya, the Khasi people don't just build bridges - they GROW them! <3\n\nThey carefully guide the roots of rubber fig trees across rivers using bamboo frames. It takes 15 to 30 years for a root bridge to become strong enough to walk across - so a person might start one that their grandchildren finish using!\n\nSome living root bridges are over about a century old, and instead of rotting away like wood, they just keep getting thicker and stronger every year. The famous Double Decker Bridge even has two crossing levels stacked on top of each other! <	"
